<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Keeping Our Communities Clean</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Should Clean the Classroom on Friday.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Describe familiar everyday activities in detail. interactive: Brainstorm questions in a group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a routine cleanup schedule (e.g., 'First, I study. Then, I clean my desk.'). Can brainstorm questions for organizing a cleanup (e.g., 'What supplies do we need?'). Can exchange information about recycling (e.g., 'When is recycling picked up?' 'It's on Monday.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a very messy classroom (or their actual classroom if applicable) on the board. Students are asked to call out what they see that is dirty or needs cleaning. (e.g., "Trash!", "Dirty desks!", "Books on the floor!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to different areas of the messy classroom picture and models simple suggestions and plans. "Look, the floor is dirty. We <b>should clean</b> the floor. I <b>can pick up</b> the trash. We <b>will clean</b> the classroom on Friday." The teacher explicitly models word stress on imperative commands like 'clean' and 'bring', and emphasizes short /ɪ/ in 'bring' and long /iː/ in 'clean'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'We <b>should</b> clean,' does it mean we *have to* clean, or it's a *good idea* to clean?" (Expected answer: "A good idea.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say '<b>Bring</b> your bag!', am I asking a question or giving an order?" (Expected answer: "Giving an order.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two students planning a classroom cleanup. The teacher reads the dialogue aloud clearly, ensuring students hear the target grammar and vocabulary in context. (e.g., "Ana: Oh no, our classroom is so dirty! Pedro: You're right. We <b>should clean</b> it on Friday. Ana: What <b>supplies</b> do we need? Pedro: We <b>will need bags</b> for <b>trash</b> and a broom. Ana: I <b>can bring</b> the broom. Pedro: Great! I <b>will collect</b> the <b>recycling</b>. When is <b>recycling</b> <b>picked up</b>? Ana: It's on Monday.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in group discussions on community actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue and respond orally or by raising hands.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: Ana and Pedro will clean the classroom on Saturday." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What will Pedro collect?" (Expected answer: "The recycling.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When is recycling picked up?" (Expected answer: "On Monday.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using modal verbs (should, can, will) for suggestions and plans; future tense; imperatives; vocabulary (clean, bring, pick up, collect, trash, recycling, supplies, desks, floor, board, organize, quick, careful, responsible, bags, tools); pronunciation of word stress and short/long vowel sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Oral Cloze/Repetition):</b> The teacher presents incomplete sentences orally or on the board. Students complete the sentences orally, focusing on pronunciation.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We ______ clean the desks." (Students say "should"). Teacher: "Say the whole sentence: 'We should clean the desks.'"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I ______ bring the cleaning supplies." (Students say "will" or "can"). Teacher: "Say the whole sentence: 'I will bring the cleaning supplies.'"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"______ up the trash!" (Students say "Pick"). Teacher: "Say: 'Pick up the trash!'" (Focus on stress for 'Pick').</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Substitution Drill in Pairs):</b> The teacher provides a base sentence and a list of words. Students take turns in pairs, substituting words to create new sentences.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Base sentence: 'We should clean the *board*.' Now use 'desks', 'floor', 'windows', 'shelves'."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "We should clean the *desks*."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "We should clean the *floor*." (And so on).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Base sentence: 'I can *collect* the recycling.' Now use 'throw', 'organize', 'bag'."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I can *throw* the trash."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction:</b> Students work in pairs. Using the messy classroom picture from the warm-up, Student A asks a question and Student B responds using target language. Then they switch roles.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What <b>should we clean</b>?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "We <b>should clean</b> the desks." / "I <b>can sweep</b> the floor."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Who <b>will bring</b> the supplies?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I <b>will bring</b> the bags." / "Pedro <b>can bring</b> the broom."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Keeping Our Communities Clean".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Role-Play: "Our Classroom Cleanup Plan".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group imagines their classroom is very dirty and they need to organize a cleanup on Friday. They must discuss and collaboratively create a plan for cleaning. They should assign tasks, suggest what needs to be done, and ask for/give information. Groups will then present their plan orally to another group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher sets the scene: "Imagine our classroom is very messy. We need to clean it on Friday. In your groups, talk about how you will clean it."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students discuss in groups, using sentence frames and vocabulary from previous stages: "We <b>should clean</b>...", "I <b>will clean</b>...", "You <b>can collect</b>...", "What <b>supplies do we need</b>?", "Who <b>will bring</b> the...?", "When is <b>recycling picked up</b>?". They brainstorm tasks (e.g., wipe desks, sweep floor, clean board, collect trash, organize books, water plants, clear shelves).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After 5-7 minutes of planning, groups present their cleanup plan to another group. They take turns explaining their assigned tasks and overall schedule. (e.g., "First, Carlos <b>will clean</b> the board. Then, Ana <b>will sweep</b> the floor. We <b>should collect</b> all the <b>trash</b> and <b>recycling</b>. We <b>will need bags</b>. Recycling is <b>picked up on Monday</b>.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussions and presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' ability to discuss, plan, and present their cleanup schedule within their groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking-focused):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of 'bring' and 'clean'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modal verbs ('should', 'can', 'will') for suggestions and plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words (e.g., trash, recycling, supplies, clean, collect, organize, team, bags, tasks, responsibility).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask and answer basic questions about tasks and schedules (e.g., "What supplies do we need?", "When is recycling picked up?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation, collaboration, and turn-taking in group discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral or written on slips):</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can make suggestions for cleaning using 'We should...'. (Yes / A little / No)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can talk about what I 'will' do to help clean. (Yes / A little / No)"</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (Oral discussion or quick poll):</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases did you use today to talk about cleaning and helping?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult when you were talking in your group today?"</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., Students were engaged in planning and used "should" and "will" effectively. Pair practice helped build confidence.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with asking 'When is...?' questions; I should provide more explicit sentence starters for question formation. Group dynamics could be improved with clearer role assignments.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job speaking about our cleanup plans! In Lesson 4, we will use these ideas and phrases to write a short paragraph about how we can keep our classroom clean. You will turn your speaking plan into a written plan."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>